  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure just offshore of Cape Cod will allow for gloomy \nconditions to continue across northern New England. A few isolated \nshowers are currently seen across western Maine on the radar. Very \nstable mid-levels should limit convective activity this afternoon, \nbut residual instability could allow for a few more isolated showers \nor thunderstorms to pop up across the interior this afternoon. \n\nTonight, calm conditions persist across NH and western Maine, with \nmarine fog and low stratus advecting into coastal areas. Fog will \ndevelop well into the interior, and continue through Wednesday \nmorning.\n\nA front will move in from the Great Lakes on Wednesday, and will \napproach NH by Wednesday evening. Most places should stay dry, but \nan stray shower or storm could form ahead of the front. The storms \nattached to the cold front itself will probably be dead on arrival \nas CAMs show a decaying trend in the storms when they are over New \nYork state. In addition, the front most likely won't cross into the \nregion until after sunset so there won't be much energy to work with \neither. Patchy fog returns Wednesday night and will continue through \nThursday morning.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nThere are a couple of features that may bring a few showers to the \nregion on Thursday. The first is a weak frontal boundary crossing \nthrough the day while the second is a shortwave crossing southern \nNew England that could bring an additional couple of showers along \nthe coast and southern NH. Most will probably not see any rain but \nthose that do will only see light amounts as QPF signals in the \nmodel guidance is limited to only a few hundredths. Otherwise, \nseasonal temperatures are expected with highs in the mid 70s to low \n80s. \n\nHigh pressure then builds in Friday and the first part of Saturday, \nkeeping the weather dry for much of the area. The high does move \noff to the northeast later Saturday as low pressure emerges off \nthe Mid-Atlantic coast, and moisture will start to increase from\nsouth to north over northern New England, potentially enough \nfor a few showers along the coast and southern NH during the day\nSaturday. But again, most will be dry as the better chances \nhold off until the second half of the weekend. Highs will in the\n70s to low 80s and lows mostly in the 50s, except some embedded\nupper 40s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\nThis initial low will keep a chance for showers along the coast and \nsouthern NH Saturday night, but rain chances further increase \nareawide Sunday into Sunday night as a 500mb approaches from \nthe west and encompasses much of the US East Coast. There's a \nstrong signal in the ensemble for most of the area to see \nmeasurable rain, although there are timing differences. If the \n500 mb trough remains an open wave the rain looks more in the \nSunday-Sunday night timeframe, but if it closes off, then a \nslower onset and end will likely be the result, making the\nhigher rain chances more in the Sunday night and Monday window.\nBy Tuesday, we should see a return to drier conditions, except \nthere may be some upslope showers in the mountains.",
